          "activity_md": "# Activity Output: TX_BBO_XX_G5_1.0_8_v1\n\n## ACTIVITY-LEVEL INFORMATION\n\n### Curricular Information\nTX_BBO_XX_G5_1.0_8_v1\n\n### Alignment\nThis activity replaces Language: Writing Similes and Metaphors (15 min.) and Activity Page 8.3 from Bluebonnet Grade 5, Unit 1, Lesson 8. It scaffolds students from identifying similes and metaphors in practice sentences to explaining why figurative language in Bertie Bowman's personal narrative meets the qualifications for each type.\n\n### Objective\nStudents will identify similes and metaphors in \"A Boy Goes to Washington\" by Bertie Bowman.\n\n### Summary of the Text\nThese two short excerpts come from *Step by Step: A Boy Goes to Washington*, a personal narrative by Bertie Bowman. In the first excerpt, thirteen-year-old Bertie has just boarded a train from South Carolina to Washington, D.C., determined to leave farm life behind. He pushes thoughts of the past out of his mind as the train moves all day through the countryside, and he offers to help the porter with any work needed. In the second excerpt, the train finally pulls into Washington at night — Bertie has never seen so many lights, and the city fills him with wonder. Both excerpts contain figurative language that shows Bowman comparing his sensory impressions to vivid, familiar things.\n\n### Link to Full Text\n*Step by Step: A Boy Goes to Washington* by Bertie Bowman — Bluebonnet Grade 5 Anthology\n\n### Excerpt Start/End\nExcerpt 1 (source paragraph 2): \"My mind was closed to all things past…\" through \"…if he needed anyone to help him.\"\nExcerpt 2 (source paragraph 10): \"When the train pulled into Washington…\" through \"…if I wanted the train job.\"\n\n### Reader Content\n**Step by Step: A Boy Goes to Washington**\n\n**Excerpt 1**\n\n(1) My mind was closed to all things past. I was not going to look back on my farm days, hog slopping, or the long hours of toil. I didn't give a thought to things back at home. That train rolled on all day long, to the song of the metal against the tracks, the blur of the towns and villages moving past my eyes. I wasn't going to get to Washington until around eight o'clock that night, and I told the porter that I would be glad to work if he needed anyone to help him.\n\n---\n\n**Excerpt 2**\n\n(2) When the train pulled into Washington, I had never seen so many lights. It was like the world was on fire. As I gathered my stuff, the porter asked me again if I wanted the train job.\n\n---\n\n## CONTENT BLOCKS: MOJO INTRODUCTION\n\n->Today we're reading excerpts from *Step by Step: A Boy Goes to Washington*, a personal narrative by Bertie Bowman. We'll explore how Bowman uses figurative language — similes and metaphors — to make his writing vivid and surprising.\n\n->Pay attention to whether a comparison uses \"like\" or \"as\" — that's the key difference between a simile and a metaphor.\n\n->Make sure you've read the excerpts from \"A Boy Goes to Washington\" on the left.\n\n---\n\n## QUESTIONS\n\n### Section 1: Driving Questions\n\n#### Question 1\n- **Question Type:** mpchoice\n- **Question:** \"Annabelle's room looked like it was hit by a hurricane.\" Is this sentence a simile or a metaphor?\n- A. Simile\n- B. Metaphor\n- C. It is not a comparison — it describes exactly what happened to the room\n- D. It is both a simile and a metaphor\n- Answer: A\n- **Distractor Rationale:**\n  - B: Students may know that both similes and metaphors involve comparisons and pick the wrong type. But the word \"like\" in \"looked like\" is the signal that makes this a simile — not a metaphor.\n  - C: Students may read \"looked like it was hit by a hurricane\" as describing the room's literal appearance. But rooms are not actually hit by hurricanes — the sentence compares how messy or chaotic the room is to hurricane damage.\n  - D: Students may think the sentence qualifies as both because it involves comparison. But simile and metaphor are mutually exclusive: whenever \"like\" or \"as\" is used, the comparison is a simile, not a metaphor.\n- **Correct Answer Rationale:** Option A is correct. The sentence uses the word \"like\" to compare Annabelle's room to a place hit by a hurricane. Using \"like\" or \"as\" to make a comparison is the defining feature of a simile.\n\n---\n\n#### Question 2\n- **Question Type:** tdq\n- **Question:** \"The snow was a blanket covering the yard.\" Is this sentence a simile or a metaphor? How do you know?\n\n**Criteria:** Student identifies this as a metaphor and explains that the sentence calls the snow a blanket without using \"like\" or \"as.\"\n\n---\n\n#### Question 3\n- **Question Type:** tdq\n- **Question:** Look at paragraph (1): \"That train rolled on all day long, to the song of the metal against the tracks.\" Is this sentence a simile or a metaphor? How do you know?\n\n**Criteria:** Student identifies this as a metaphor and explains that calling the sound of the tracks a \"song\" is a comparison that does not use \"like\" or \"as.\"\n\n---\n\n#### Question 4\n- **Question Type:** tdq\n- **Question:** Using the two criteria for a metaphor — (1) it compares two things and (2) it does not use \"like\" or \"as\" — explain why the sentence from paragraph (1) meets both qualifications.\n\n**Criteria:** Student explains that the sentence compares the sound of the train tracks to a song (two things being compared) and that the comparison does not use \"like\" or \"as.\"\n\n---\n\n### CONTENT BLOCKS: TYPE 2 (TRANSITION TO TT)\n\n->Now that we've practiced identifying similes and metaphors, let's put everything together with one final question about the text.\n\n---\n\n### Section 2: Assessment\n\n#### Assessment — Target Task (TDQ Target Task ELEM)\n\n- **Question Type:** tdq\n- **Question:** Look at paragraph (2): \"It was like the world was on fire.\" Using the two criteria for a simile — (1) it compares two things and (2) it uses \"like\" or \"as\" — explain why this sentence meets both qualifications.\n\n**Criteria:** Student explains that the sentence compares the lights of Washington to a world on fire (two things being compared) and uses the word \"like.\"\n\n---\n\n#### Extension Question\n- **Question Type:** extension\n- **Question:** Bertie writes that the lights of Washington looked like \"the world was on fire.\" If you were arriving somewhere for the first time, what simile or metaphor would you use to describe what you saw?\n\n**Criteria:** Student offers a simile or metaphor describing a first-arrival or new-experience setting and can briefly explain what two things are being compared.\n\n---\n\n### CONTENT BLOCKS: TYPE 3 (CONCLUSION)\n\n->Nice work today! You identified similes and metaphors by checking two things: whether a comparison uses \"like\" or \"as,\" and whether two different things are being compared.\n\n->Now you can spot similes and metaphors in any text. Next time you're reading, look for comparisons — they're often where an author's most vivid language lives.\n",
